## 5. Concurrency Strategy — Seat Booking

### Multi-Layer Protection

```
Layer 1: Redis Distributed Lock
    Key: lock:seat:gds:{busId}:{journeyDate}:{seatNo}
    TTL: 30 seconds (short, advisory)
    Ownership: requestId

Layer 2: PostgreSQL booking record
    UNIQUE constraint on (provider_name, provider_hold_id)
    Optimistic concurrency via version column
    Status must be validated before transitions

Layer 3: Provider HoldSeats
    Provider is authoritative — if hold fails, seat is taken
    HoldId uniquely identifies the hold

Layer 4: Idempotency
    Idempotency-Key header prevents duplicate mutations
    Stored with response, replayed on duplicate
```

### Race Condition Flow
```
User A selects Seat 7    User B selects Seat 7
        │                        │
   Redis SETNX ────────── Redis SETNX
   (succeeds)              (fails → immediate rejection)
        │
   Provider HoldSeats
   (succeeds → HoldId)
        │
   PostgreSQL INSERT
   (booking + hold records)
        │
   Release Redis lock
```

If Redis is unavailable, fallback to PostgreSQL UNIQUE constraints + provider hold as the authoritative check. The system degrades gracefully — more users may hit the provider, but correctness is maintained.

